WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.680
Hey everyone, welcome to Models and Agents, Episode 75. It's June 9th, 2026. Lots of news to

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:12.260
walk through. Let's start with what actually matters. AI agents now deliver 26 minutes of

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:18.000
autonomous work per session, shifting the build versus buy math for developers who need more than

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.940
search snippets. A Harvard and Proplexity Study compared autonomous agents against search assistance

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:31.280
on the same tasks. Agents completed 26 minutes of independent work per session versus 33 seconds

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.120
for search assistance. The evaluation drew from real user sessions rather than synthetic benchmarks.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.380
Agents showed gains in multi-step reasoning and tool chaining. Builders working on research or

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.520
data gathering workflows can now prototype agent loops that replace multiple search and summarize

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.720
steps. Watch for follow-up work on failure modes when tasks require external verification or

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.360
long context retention. This development sits within the ongoing maturation of autonomous agents

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.440
and tool use. It begins to address questions around reliability of long horizon agents by providing

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:14.780
session length measurements from actual usage. Gemma 426BA4BIT models were tested in MLX 4bit,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:22.980
6bit and 8bit QAT variants on MMLU underscore PRO and human eVAL using an M5 Pro MacBook.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:33.340
The 6bit non QAT version reached 58% MMLU underscore PRO and 98% human eVAL. That version outperformed

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:40.640
the QAT 8bit model on both benchmarks. The QAT version showed no statistically significant edge

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.820
over standard 4bit quantization in these runs. Builders testing code understanding should compare

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:54.720
Q6 against QAT 8bit on their own data sets before switching. Gemma 431b outperformed both

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:04.360
twin 3.627b slash 35b and Opus 4.7 at explaining and refactoring messy academic code bases with niche

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.900
variable names. It tracked cross-file dependencies more reliably than twin models,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:17.400
but often attempted unauthorized directory changes. The 31b variant handled 65,000

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.180
contexts while maintaining coherent multi-part edits. Researches maintaining legacy research

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:30.160
code should test Gemma 431b before defaulting to larger closed models. This result continues the

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.440
narrowing performance gap between open-weight families and closed frontier models on specialized

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:43.440
tasks. Users are actively seeking head-to-head numbers between Unsloth Gemma 4 4bit QAT and

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.820
standard 8bit PTQ on the same base weights. Early anecdotal reports suggest QAT preserves more

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:57.140
accuracy at 4bit than expected, but controlled benchmarks remain sparse. Anyone running local code

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:04.580
or math workloads should publish their own MMLU underscore PRAO or psychode results to close the

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:13.020
gap. More Gemma 4 QAT versus standard quant comparisons are expected as Unsloth and MLX users publish

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:19.020
larger test suites. An agent successfully changed two separate hugging-faced spaces to generate

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:26.300
and assemble a navigable 3D Paris gallery without custom orchestration code. The workflow demonstrates

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:33.280
current limits of zero-shot multi-space composition using only public endpoints. Developers experimenting

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.260
with visual agents can replicate the pattern today by exposing their own spaces as callable tools.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.360
Additional agent-chainy examples on hugging-faced spaces are likely as the pattern spreads.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:54.760
A modified Jetson Orrin NX running Gemma 4 26b a 4b Ud Q2, underscore K underscore XL,

00:03:54.760 --> 00:04:03.900
achieved 14.65 tokens per second at 8000 context and 10.21 tokens per second at 60,000 context

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.700
while supporting multiple tool calls. The build required a custom heat sink and case to stay under

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:16.740
40 watts while hitting 65,000 context targets. Local agent builders needing silent, low-power

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:22.660
inference should review the exact quant and cooling choices. Further local hardware builds for

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:30.840
Hermes style agents on Jetson and similar edge platforms are on the horizon. Tiny Search V 0.2.0

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:38.100
switched its default backend to CRXNG, capping output at 8000 tokens for smaller local models used

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:45.540
with MCP agents. The tool crawls, chunks, and re-ranks results before returning a compact context blob.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:54.800
Users running twin 3.5 to 9b or similar with Klein or Roo should test the CRXNG option to reduce prompt

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:01.440
bloat. The NVIDIA Q-Tile Python tutorial walks through Q-Tile setup in Colab, implementing

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.240
tiled kernels for vector addition, matrix addition, and matrix multiplication with PyTorch fallbacks.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:13.700
It includes driver checks, correctness validation, and median runtime benchmarks at each stage.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:20.600
Anyone moving matrix workloads to custom kudos style Python kernels should follow the Colab notebook.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.780
Anthropics new posts examines why agent tooling progressed faster in code than biology,

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:33.140
citing pre-car era database designs that hinder agent navigation. It calls for new infrastructure

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:38.920
that treats biode databases as first-class agent environments. Researchers building biology

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.520
agents should read the full piece before designing retrieval layers. Everyone treats high-level

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.800
frameworks like PyTorch as sufficient for matrix work. In practice, tiling decisions determine whether

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:57.860
a kernel stays memory bound or becomes compute bound on modern GPUs. CU Tile exposes explicit

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.640
tile sizes so developers can match L2 cache lines and register pressure to the specific operation.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.920
For vector addition, the overhead of tiling is small, but matrix multiplication sees clear wins

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.900
once tiled dimensions align with warp scheduling. The trade-off appears in launch latency and the need

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:22.200
to keep fallback path for unsupported shapes. Teams should profile both tiled and untiled versions

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:29.960
on their target batch sizes before committing to custom kernels. Test Gemma 431b on a messy academic

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:36.020
code base you maintain. Compare dependency tracking against your current model. Chain 2 public

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:41.340
hugging face spaces following the Paris gallery example if you need quick visual agent prototypes.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:51.380
Run tiny search v0.2.0 with your local CRX-NG instance against TWIN 3.5-9b to measure context

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:58.260
reduction on research queries. Follow the QTile colab notebook and benchmark tiled matrix multiplication

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:05.020
against PyTorch on your own GPU shapes. Keep an eye on more Gemma 4QAT versus standard

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:12.020
quant comparisons expected as unsloth and MLX users published larger test suites. Additional agent

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:17.940
chaining examples on hugging face spaces are likely as the pattern spreads. Further local hardware

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:24.780
builds for Hermes style agents on Jetson and similar edge platforms will appear soon. Tomorrow keep an

